{
  "id": "c19",
  "title": "Bits and Bytes",
  "lessons": [
    {
      "id": "c19-l1",
      "title": "Binary and Hexadecimal",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "Inside the machine, every number is bits: 13 is 1101 in binary (8 + 4 + 1). Eight bits make a byte, which holds 0 to 255.",
        "Long rows of bits are hard to read, so programmers group them: hexadecimal uses 16 digits (0–9, a–f), one per 4 bits. In C, 0xFF is 255. printf shows hex with %x.",
        "The PDP-11 crowd loved octal (base 8, one digit per 3 bits): a number starting with 0 is octal in C, so 017 is 15, and %o prints octal. Unix file permissions like 0755 are still written that way."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int n = 13, b;",
          "    printf(\"%d in hex: %x, in octal: %o\\n\", n, n, n);",
          "    printf(\"0xFF = %d, 0755 = %d\\n\", 0xFF, 0755);",
          "    printf(\"13 in binary: \");",
          "    for (b = 3; b >= 0; b--)",
          "        printf(\"%d\", (n >> b) & 1);",
          "    printf(\"\\n\");",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "13 in hex: d, in octal: 15",
          "0xFF = 255, 0755 = 493",
          "13 in binary: 1101"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
