    },
    {
      "id": "c19-l2",
      "title": "& | ^ and Shifts",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "C can work on single bits. a & b keeps the bits that are 1 in both, a | b the bits that are 1 in either, and a ^ b (XOR) the bits where they differ. ~a flips every bit.",
        "a << 1 moves all bits one place left, which doubles the number; a >> 1 moves them right, which halves it. 1 << n is a number with only bit n set.",
        "Don't mix up & with &&, or | with ||: the doubled ones are for true and false, the single ones work bit by bit."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    int a = 12, b = 10;",
          "    printf(\"a & b = %d\\n\", a & b);",
          "    printf(\"a | b = %d\\n\", a | b);",
          "    printf(\"a ^ b = %d\\n\", a ^ b);",
          "    printf(\"a << 2 = %d\\n\", a << 2);",
          "    printf(\"a >> 1 = %d\\n\", a >> 1);",
          "    printf(\"1 << 5 = %d\\n\", 1 << 5);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "a & b = 8",
          "a | b = 14",
          "a ^ b = 6",
          "a << 2 = 48",
          "a >> 1 = 6",
          "1 << 5 = 32"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
          "id": "c19-l2-e1",
          "type": "multiple_choice",
          "prompt": "What is 6 & 3? (110 and 011)",
          "options": [
